@@ -881,8 +881,9 @@ Counterfactual transparency---a small $\Theta_i(g)$, ideally a singleton---makes
 At the discovery record ($T=2$, diagonal states served), each firm holds two tokens sharing the profile $(0,1)$ but differing in payoff: firm~1 won at $s_{00}$ and lost at $s_{11}$, firm~2 the reverse.
 Because mechanisms are deterministic, the conflicting payoffs \emph{certify} the two tokens as distinct states (\cref{rem:certification}), and the agent's best coherent grouping splits them: the winning token pins its architecture as preferred (current action stays optimal, contributing zero), the losing token pins the other architecture (switching to it gains $\tfrac12$ against every rival action---a differentiated loss becomes a pooled half, or a pooled half a differentiated win).
 The two tokens average a per-token gain of $(\tfrac12+0)/2=\tfrac14$, so $\widehat B_i=(T/\lvert r_i\rvert)\sum_t\Delta=(2/2)\cdot \tfrac12=\tfrac12$ for both firms---identical leverage from opposite experiences---so each elects iff $\lambda_i^{(0)}\widehat B_i\geq\gamma_i$, i.e.\ $\lambda_i^{(0)}/2\geq\gamma_i$.
+Acting on the row refinement, once acquired, means re-differentiating by row at the switching hurdle $\kappa_i$; because transparency makes the realized horizon-$T$ gain equal to $\widehat B_i=\tfrac12$, the firm re-differentiates when $\kappa_i<\tfrac12$ and---knowing the row law yet finding the change not worth its cost---holds its old policy when $\kappa_i>\tfrac12$, the deterministic face of enlightened inertia (\cref{rem:stickiness}).
 The valuation used no conjecture about the rival and no reference to which refinement might arrive; transparency (winner-take-all) makes each $\Theta_i(g)$ a singleton, so the deliberative estimate is exact.
